 ],
 "literal": "To enemies joy to become, distressingly / in this manner would they come, O lord of the honey-flowing shore? / As the small tongue of the bright bell sounds a sad melody, / the wheel rim of the tall chariot drawn by swift horses passed over, / like the neytal flower of the dark backwater, / she suffered, the pitiable one, she from whom you parted.",
 "translation": "O lord of the honey-flowing shore! Would anyone return in this manner, causing distress to her and bringing joy to her enemies? Like the neytal flower in the dark backwater crushed by the wheel rim of a tall chariot drawn by swift horses, while its bright bells with small tongues ring a sad melody, she from whom you parted has suffered, the pitiable one.",
 "modern": "தேன் பாய்கின்ற துறைமுகத்தின் தலைவனே! பகைவர்களுக்கு மகிழ்ச்சி உண்டாகும் படியாக, துன்பம் தரும் வகையில் இப்படி வருவார்களா? வேகமான குதிரைகள் பூட்டிய நெடிய தேரின் சக்கரம் ஏறி நசுக்கியதால், சிறிய நாக்கைக் கொண்ட விளங்கும் மணிகள் விளரிப் பண் போல ஒலிக்க, கரிய உப்பங்கழியில் உள்ள நெய்தல் மலர் வருந்தியது போல, நீ பிரிந்து சென்ற உனது காதலி மிகவும் வருந்தினாள்; அவள் இரங்கத்தக்கவள்.",
 "explanation": "The friend (தோழி) speaks to the hero (தலைவன்) upon his return, describing the intense suffering the heroine (தலைவி) endured during his absence. She compares the heroine to a delicate neytal flower in the dark backwater that has been crushed under the heavy wheels of his swift chariot. The ringing of the chariot's bells is compared to the mournful viḷari tune, symbolizing the sadness of her state. The friend gently reproaches him, asking if it is right to return in a manner that causes such distress, which only gives joy to those who wish them ill. Although the colophon lists the tiṇai as Kuriñci, the imagery of the shore (துறைவ), backwater (கழி), and neytal flower belongs to the Neytal (coastal) landscape, used here to evoke the theme of anxious waiting and separation.",
